From various layers of sedimentary rocks, 159 remains of fossil decapod crustaceans (animals such as crabs) were collected from a quarry near the village of Lisowice in southern Poland. They lived during the age of Late Jurassic called early Kimmeridgian, approximately 155–153 m.y.a. Of these remains, 78 fossil carapaces (protective shells that cover decapod’s bodies) were assigned to 14 species in nine genera. Two of the investigated layers, which were especially rich in fossils, represent different types of ancient environments: one was near a sponge biostrome, the second was a sponge biostrome itself. Communities of different decapod crustaceans inhabited these environments. Additionally, the locality of Lisowice is characterized by the highest number of different decapod species known from any place in Europe that is early Kimmeridgian in age. The study also reports four species that have never been found in Poland and shows that eight species lived earlier or later than previously known.
